<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by Tauri (bundle.macOS looks for this file next to
  tauri.conf.json). Everything here is a macOS integration point Tauri's config cannot express.

  WHAT THIS DOES: registers the app as something a FOLDER can be opened with, so Finder offers
  "Open With ▸ YggShell" on a directory, and `open -a YggShell <dir>` works from a shell — which is
  what the `ygg` launcher is built on.

  WHY `public.folder` AND NOT `ext: ["*"]`: Tauri's fileAssociations only speak in extensions, and a
  wildcard there would register this app as an opener for EVERY file on the machine — it would appear
  in every "Open With" menu in the system. A UTI says what is actually meant: folders, nothing else.

  WHAT THE DOCUMENT TYPE DOES AND DOES NOT BUY. It is what makes `open -a YggShell <dir>` work, which
  is what the `ygg` launcher is built on. It does NOT put an entry in Finder's context menu: Finder
  offers no "Open With" submenu for a FOLDER at all — it opens folders itself. That is why the
  NSServices block below exists, and it is the same mechanism iTerm2 uses ("New iTerm2 Tab Here",
  NSMessage: openTab). Measured, not assumed.

  THE SERVICE HAS TWO HALVES. This declaration says what the menu item is called and what it accepts;
  src/services.rs registers the object holding the method NSMessage names. Without that half the
  entry appears and does nothing, which is worse than not appearing — so the selector is pinned by a
  test that reads this file.

  LSHandlerRank Alternate: the app offers itself, it does not claim to be the default handler for
  folders. Finder is. Taking that over would be a hostile thing for a terminal to do.

  The path a folder arrives on is validated in src/launch.rs before anything acts on it: it is a
  working directory for a shell the settings choose, never a command line (ADR-PROJ-001 §5).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSServices</key>
    <array>
      <dict>
        <key>NSMenuItem</key>
        <dict>
          <key>default</key>
          <string>New YggShell Terminal Here</string>
        </dict>
        <key>NSMessage</key>
        <string>openTerminalHere</string>
        <key>NSPortName</key>
        <string>YggShell</string>
        <key>NSRequiredContext</key>
        <dict>
          <key>NSTextContent</key>
          <string>FilePath</string>
        </dict>
        <!--
          Copied from Apple's own Terminal.app ("New Terminal at Folder"), which is the exact case
          this is for: a right-click on the EMPTY AREA of a Finder window, meaning "a terminal in the
          folder I am looking at". Measured in its Info.plist rather than guessed.

          `public.directory`, not `public.folder`: the folder type does not produce the entry in that
          empty-area menu. `com.apple.resolvable` is what makes it work on an alias or a symlink.

          FILES ARE ACCEPTED TOO, via `public.item`, and the reason is the one thing this must not get
          wrong: the file is never OPENED. `launch::resolve` turns it into the directory holding it,
          so right-clicking a script means "a terminal where that script lives" — which is how a
          terminal has always treated a file dropped on it, and what iTerm2's entry does as well.

          `public.item` alone would not be enough: `public.directory` is what produces the entry when
          the right-click lands on a window's EMPTY AREA, where there is no selection at all.
        -->
        <key>NSSendFileTypes</key>
        <array>
          <string>public.directory</string>
          <string>com.apple.resolvable</string>
          <string>public.item</string>
        </array>
        <key>NSSendTypes</key>
        <array>
          <string>public.plain-text</string>
        </array>
      </dict>
    </array>
    <key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
    <array>
      <dict>
        <key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
        <string>Folder</string>
        <key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
        <string>Editor</string>
        <key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
        <array>
          <string>public.folder</string>
        </array>
      </dict>
    </array>
  </dict>
</plist>
